    "text": "To find the sum of all the positive divisors of 91, we first need to find its prime factorization. We can do this by dividing 91 by the smallest prime number, which is 2. Since 91 is not divisible by 2, we move on to the next prime number, which is 3. We can see that 91 is not divisible by 3 either. We continue this process until we find a prime number that divides 91. In this case, we find that 91 is divisible by 7, and we get 91 = 7 * 13. Since 7 and 13 are both prime numbers, we have found the prime factorization of 91: 91 = 7 * 13.\n\nNow that we have the prime factorization of 91, we can use it to find the sum of all its positive divisors. The formula for the sum of the divisors of a number is:\n\nSum of divisors = (p1^(a1+1) - 1)/(p1 - 1) * (p2^(a2+1) - 1)/(p2 - 1) * ... * (pk^(ak+1) - 1)/(pk - 1)\n\nwhere p1, p2, ..., pk are the prime factors of the number, and a1, a2, ..., ak are their respective exponents.\n\nIn our case, the prime factors of 91 are 7 and 13, and their exponents are both 1. Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\nSum of divisors = (7^(1+1) - 1)/(7 - 1) * (13^(1+1) - 1)/(13 - 1)\n= (7^2 - 1)/(7 - 1) * (13^2 - 1)/(13 - 1)\n= (49 - 1)/(7 - 1) * (169 - 1)/(13 - 1)\n= 48/6 * 168/12\n= 8 * 14\n= 112\n\nTherefore, the sum of all the positive divisors of 91 is 112.",
